EXT. RIVERBOAT SAWMILL - DAY

A barge-mounted sawmill moored on a brown river. A huge blade whines through a log. Sawdust drifts like snow. Men shout from the lower deck.

MARSHAL EVANDER COLE, 40s, dusty badge, stands at the rail with his thumbs in his belt. TILDA HASKELL, 30s, in a leather apron, feeds logs to the blade. RAWLINS DUNMORE, 40s, mill foreman, glowers from the catwalk. Below the saw, JOAQUIN SPARROW, 20s, a wiry deckhand, hauls a rope.

EVANDER
Mr. Dunmore. I'd like a word.

RAWLINS
I'm busy.

EVANDER
So's the river.

Rawlins comes down the steps, wiping his hands. Tilda cuts the saw. The silence is sudden.

RAWLINS
What's this about, Marshal?

EVANDER
A dead man, found downstream. Pinned under a log from this barge.

RAWLINS
Accidents happen.

EVANDER
This one had a bullet in him.

Tilda sets down her lever. Joaquin lets go of the rope. Rawlins keeps his hands where Evander can see them.

RAWLINS
I know nothing about it.

EVANDER
Then you won't mind if I look at the log.

RAWLINS
Be my guest.

He walks to the stack. Evander follows. Joaquin darts for the gangplank, but Tilda catches him by the collar and drags him back. He twists loose and sprints across the deck. Evander draws his revolver.

EVANDER
Joaquin!

JOAQUIN
It wasn't me!

EVANDER
I never said it was.

Joaquin stops at the rail. His shoulders heave. Rawlins backs away, boots scraping on the planks.

TILDA
(quietly)
Joaquin, tell him.

JOAQUIN
He made me, Tilda.

RAWLINS
Shut your mouth, boy.

JOAQUIN
He shot Mr. Pell over the lumber contract and made me drag the body to the saw. I didn't want to. I swear I didn't.

RAWLINS
That's a lie.

EVANDER
Is it?

RAWLINS
Marshal, I've run this mill twenty years.

EVANDER
Then twenty years is plenty.

Rawlins lunges for the gun on his hip. Evander fires first. Rawlins spins and falls across a pile of sawdust, clutching his shoulder. Tilda steps over him, kicks the gun away, and stands there breathing.

TILDA
He's alive.

EVANDER
Good. He can hang proper.
